    881	| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 5 (FINAL WRAP) — theoretical wrap: lift slot-1 heuristic named-exponent corollary to a CONDITIONAL THEOREM under RH + Montgomery's pair-correlation conjecture, by adapting Goldston-Montgomery 1987 ("Pair correlation of zeros and primes in short intervals") bilinear-form analysis to the truncated-zero-sum test function | THEORY (rigor work; conditional theorem under explicit hypothesis with explicit valid range; algorithmic corollary as precise conditional theorem; what's NOT proved made explicit) | I (E1.5 + S195 + S196 + S202 + S240 + S241 + S242 + S243) — partial-positive (CONDITIONAL on Montgomery) | Thread 7 commit step 5/5 FINAL (S244). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/slot5_theorem.md` (~330 lines): 14 sections covering theorem statement (Theorem A and Corollary B), setup and notation, asymptotic kernel R(y^rho) = y^rho/(rho log y) (1 + O(y^{-1/4} + 1/(gamma_j log y))), variance integral bilinear-form expansion, diagonal evaluation D_K = log^2 K / (4 pi^2 K) (1 + O(1/log K)) UNCONDITIONAL (Riemann-von Mangoldt zero density), off-diagonal residual R_K(X, H) split into close-pair (Montgomery-needed) and far-pair (RH-only) bounds, full proof of Theorem A combining diagonal + bounded off-diagonal, proof of Corollary B by substitution K = ceil((log x)^{2(beta-1)}). **Theorem A (slot 5, conditional on RH + Montgomery)**: for H in [X^epsilon, X log^{-2} X] and K in [log^2 X, X^{1-epsilon}], (1/H) integral_X^{X+H} (pi(y) - pi_K(y))^2 dy = (1+o(1)) X log^2 K / (2 pi^2 K log^2 X). **Corollary B (slot 5, algorithmic; conditional on same)**: for any beta > 1, K = ceil((log x)^{2(beta-1)}) gives polylog-time algorithm with L^2-typical error eps_typ(x) <= (1+o(1)) (beta-1) sqrt(2) sqrt(x) log log x / (pi log^beta x). **Explicit valid range (★): X^epsilon <= H <= X log^{-2} X, log^2 X <= K <= X^{1-epsilon}**. **Role of Montgomery**: only used for the close-pair off-diagonal bound (Wigner repulsion suppresses pair count at scale 1/H). Far-pair bound is RH-only. **Under RH alone**: same proof gives sigma^2_RH <= X log^2 K log^2 log K / (2 pi^2 K log^2 X) — same exponent in log X, log^2 log K factor weaker. Equivalently eps_typ^RH(x) << sqrt(x) (log log x)^2 / log^{1+alpha/2} x — same named exponent, log^2 log x factor worse than under Montgomery. **What slot 5 produces NEW**: (i) conditional theorem statement with explicit valid range; (ii) exact role of Montgomery (close-pair only); (iii) RH-only fallback bound with named log^2 log K factor; (iv) polylog-time algorithm corollary as a precise conditional theorem (not heuristic). **What slot 5 does NOT prove**: (i) worst-case (pointwise in y) bound — Theorem A is L^2-typical; half-Gaussian shape (S241) suggests pointwise sqrt(log K) larger than typical at the tail; (ii) unconditional version — Montgomery itself is open; (iii) effective constants beyond asymptotic — (1+o(1)) packages the GUE 0.74 factor (S195/S243 F_GUE = 0.55 measurement) but does not isolate it. **Comparison to literature**: Riemann-Riesel (explicit formula); Montgomery 1973 (pair correlation); Odlyzko 1989 / Hejhal 1994 (numerical GUE verification); Goldston-Montgomery 1987 (canonical conditional variance theorem; slot 5's proof technique adapts their bilinear expansion to a different test function); Goldston 1990 (unconditional RH-only bound); Galway 2004 (unconditional pi(x) algorithm O(x^{1/2+epsilon})); Hiary 2011 (zero precomputation); S195 (sigma-formula derivation under random-phase, rigorised here); S196 / S202 (Thread 3 closure conditional on Montgomery — slot 5 lifts the same hypothesis to partial-positive direction); S224 (Correlation Dichotomy partial-positive template); S240 (slot 1 heuristic named-exponent corollary, rigorised here); S241 (slot 2 multi-sample empirical support — half-Gaussian shape implies tail behaviour controlled by Theorem A); S242 + S243 (slots 3+4 kernel-axis closure, 17 kernels 180 cells; combined with Theorem A says algorithmic bound is kernel-optimal in second-moment regime). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Goldston-Montgomery 1987 bilinear-form analysis. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md entry promoted from USED-E (S195) + USED-I (S240) to USED-T (conditional theorem statement, S244). **Falsifiers**: (1) multi-sample empirical run at x >= 10^12 with sigma^2_eff exceeding (T-A) prediction by > 2x — directly tested at 10^9, sigma_eff/sigma_pred = 0.74, asymptotic correct up to GUE constant; (2) rigorous proof under just RH that gives full sigma-formula without Montgomery — would imply log^2 log K loss is unnecessary; (3) sharper pair-correlation conjecture refining (1+o(1)) to match empirical 0.55 factor — would tighten constant. **Thread 7 status**: DONE_PARTIAL_POSITIVE_CONDITIONAL. Aggregate Thread 7 contribution: polylog-time algorithm for approximate pi(x) with named-exponent error eps(x) <= sqrt(x) log log x / log^beta x for any beta > 1, conditional on RH + Montgomery's pair-correlation conjecture. Empirically verified across 3 decades (S241), kernel-optimal across 17 kernel families (S242 + S243), rigorised modulo Montgomery (S244, this slot). **First A-shape positive-direction CONDITIONAL theorem on an adjacent pi-related computation produced by the project**. **Slot self-grade**: B (rigor work; conditional theorem under unproven hypothesis; not A because Goldston-Montgomery 1987 supplies the engine; slot's contribution is the precise polylog-K specialisation, the algorithmic corollary, and the explicit valid range). **Recommended next thread**: ESCALATE TO USER — five-thread frontier fully traversed (Threads 1-4 closed per-query polylog frontier, Thread 5 closed A-grade-shaped partial-positive Correlation Dichotomy, Thread 6 closed B-NEGATIVE P1 batched-on-q, Thread 7 closed B-PARTIAL-POSITIVE-CONDITIONAL). Future commit slots should pick from OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md remaining candidates (P2 prime gap function batched on h, P4 twin-prime narrow-window,